WEBVTT
NOTE OpenIE Research TV v3.0 — game-engines (45s) Narrator Charlie

1
00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:03.124
First, with exact whole-number physics,

2
00:00:03.124 --> 00:00:09.467
one game blueprint replays identically across Windows, macOS, Linux, and major engines.

3
00:00:09.907 --> 00:00:18.080
Second, standard decimal math drifts across operating systems within thirty-seven frames, splitting chaotic collisions apart.

4
00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:21.777
Third, graphics cards differ slightly on pixels,

5
00:00:21.777 --> 00:00:26.120
so rendering sits safely behind a one-way visual tolerance gate.

6
00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:33.216
In a chaotic billiard break, a microscopic decimal rounding gap between Windows and macOS snowballs into

7
00:00:33.216 --> 00:00:40.705
a totally different game within seconds. Whole-number physics locks the score, while graphics cards paint the screen.

8
00:00:41.145 --> 00:00:46.853
Writing game rules once as a checked blueprint cuts Unity C-sharp code seventy-six percent,

9
00:00:46.853 --> 00:00:52.687
steps ten thousand actors in fifty-eight microseconds, and replays hour-long matches cleanly.
